#: ../../../error_handling.rst:4
msgid "Subprocess Error Handling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:6
msgid ""
"Xonsh treats shell commands as first-class code.  When a command fails, you "
"usually want your script to **stop** instead of silently marching past the "
"failure — the way a Python exception would — but you also want the "
"flexibility of ``&&``/``||`` short-circuit logic that the shell is built "
"around."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:12
msgid ""
"This page walks through the rules xonsh uses to decide *when* a failing "
"subprocess raises a ``subprocess.CalledProcessError``, how those rules "
"interact with pipes, logical operators, captured forms and per-command "
"decorators, and how the interactive prompt displays (or hides) the resulting "
"exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:20
msgid "Chains"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:22
msgid ""
"To describe where exceptions are raised we use the word **chain** for a "
"grouping of subprocess commands that produces a single result:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:25
msgid "**single chain** — one plain command: ``ls file``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:26
msgid "**pipe chain** — commands joined with ``|``: ``echo 1 | grep 1 | head``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:27
msgid ""
"**logical chain** — commands joined with ``&&``/``||``: ``ls file1 || ls "
"file2 || echo gone``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:30
msgid ""
"Statements separated by ``;`` or newlines are *independent* chains. For "
"example, ``echo 1 && echo 2 ; echo 3 ; echo 1 | grep 1`` contains **three** "
"chains."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:34
msgid ""
"Xonsh decides whether to raise *per chain*, not per individual command, "
"which is what gives ``||``/``&&`` their shell-like rescue semantics:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:37
msgid ""
"@ ls /no || echo rescued            # one chain, rescued by ||\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"rescued                              # no exception\n"
"\n"
"@ echo 1 && ls /no && echo never    # one chain, ends on a failing ls\n"
"1\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"# CalledProcessError (the chain as a whole failed)\n"
"\n"
"@ echo a ; ls /no ; echo b           # three chains; second fails, third "
"never runs\n"
"a\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"# CalledProcessError on the second chain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:55
msgid "Environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:57
msgid ""
"Xonsh exposes three knobs for error handling.  The first two control "
"**whether** a subprocess failure raises; the third controls **display of the "
"exception** at the interactive prompt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:62
msgid "``$XONSH_SUBPROC_RAISE_ERROR`` — default ``True``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:64
msgid ""
"Raises ``subprocess.CalledProcessError`` when the **final result of a "
"chain** is a non-zero exit code.  This is the rule that makes xonsh scripts "
"behave like Python: a failing command stops execution unless you explicitly "
"rescue it with ``||`` or ``@error_ignore``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:69
msgid "``ls /no`` → raises (single chain, failed)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:70
msgid "``ls /no | grep root`` → raises (pipe chain, final stage failed)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:71
msgid "``ls /no || echo ok`` → **no** raise (logical chain rescued by ``||``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:72
msgid "``echo 1 && ls /no`` → raises (chain ends on failing ``ls``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:73
msgid ""
"``(echo 1 && ls /etc) || echo fb`` → no raise (outer ``||`` rescued by a "
"successful inner chain)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:77
msgid "``$XONSH_SUBPROC_CMD_RAISE_ERROR`` — default ``False``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:79
msgid ""
"Raises on **every** failing command regardless of chain context — any non-"
"zero exit is fatal.  When ``True`` it short-circuits chain semantics: ``ls /"
"no || echo fb`` would raise on ``ls``, the ``||`` fallback never runs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:84
msgid ""
"Reserved for scripts that really want \"fail fast on anything\".  The old "
"name ``$RAISE_SUBPROC_ERROR`` is kept as a deprecated alias that syncs to "
"``XONSH_SUBPROC_CMD_RAISE_ERROR``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:89
msgid "``$XONSH_PROMPT_SHOW_SUBPROC_ERROR`` — default ``False``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:91
msgid ""
"This is a **display** flag — it does not change whether an exception is "
"raised, only whether the **interactive prompt** prints xonsh's ``subprocess."
"CalledProcessError: ...`` line after the command's own ``stderr``.  The "
"command's own ``stderr`` is always visible because the subprocess writes it "
"directly to the terminal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:97
msgid ""
"``False`` *(default)* — interactive prompt stays quiet after a failed "
"command.  You see the command's ``stderr``, the prompt returns, and "
"``$LAST_RETURN_CODE`` reflects the failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:100
msgid ""
"``True`` — restores the historical behavior of printing ``subprocess."
"CalledProcessError: Command '...' returned non-zero exit status N.`` under "
"the command's own output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:104
msgid ""
"Non-interactive scripts (``./script.xsh``, ``xonsh -c``) are **unaffected** "
"— they always show the exception, because in script mode you usually want to "
"know which line blew up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:108
msgid ""
"The per-command ``@error_raise`` decorator **always** shows the exception "
"regardless of this flag — it is the explicit per-command opt-in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:114
msgid "Captured subprocess ``!()`` is the only exemption"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:116
msgid ""
"Every subprocess form raises on a non-zero return code by default — bare "
"commands, ``![...]``, ``$[...]``, ``$(...)``, ``@$(...)``.  The **only** "
"exception is the full-capture form ``!(...)``: it returns a "
"``CommandPipeline`` object and xonsh leaves error handling entirely up to "
"you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:122
msgid ""
"@ ls nofile            # exception\n"
"@ $(ls nofile)         # exception\n"
"@ $[ls nofile]         # exception\n"
"@ ![ls nofile]         # exception\n"
"\n"
"@ !(ls nofile)         # no exception — returns a CommandPipeline"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:131
msgid ""
"``!(...)`` is designed to be tested directly, because ``CommandPipeline`` is "
"truthy when the command succeeded and falsy when it failed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:135
msgid ""
"@ if !(ls nofile):\n"
"      print(\"found\")\n"
"  else:\n"
"      print(\"absent\")\n"
"absent"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:143
msgid ""
"If you want a specific ``!(...)`` call to raise anyway, use the "
"``@error_raise`` decorator inside it — the decorator wins over the ``!()`` "
"exemption:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:147
msgid ""
"@ if !(@error_raise ls nofile):\n"
"      print(\"found\")\n"
"# CalledProcessError — @error_raise overrides the !() exemption"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:155
msgid "Per-command decorators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:157
msgid ""
"Two decorator aliases give you an escape hatch that is scoped to a single "
"command inside a larger chain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:161
msgid "``@error_raise`` — always raise"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:163
msgid ""
"Force ``subprocess.CalledProcessError`` on this command, regardless of "
"``$XONSH_SUBPROC_RAISE_ERROR``, ``$XONSH_SUBPROC_CMD_RAISE_ERROR``, or "
"whether the command sits inside a normally-rescuing chain:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:167
msgid ""
"@ @error_raise ls /no || echo fb\n"
"# CalledProcessError — @error_raise wins over ||\n"
"\n"
"@ !(@error_raise ls /no)\n"
"# CalledProcessError — @error_raise wins over !() exemption too"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:175
msgid ""
"It also **always** shows the exception at the interactive prompt, overriding "
"``$XONSH_PROMPT_SHOW_SUBPROC_ERROR = False``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:179
msgid "``@error_ignore`` — never raise"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:181
msgid ""
"The inverse — the command *never* raises, no matter the environment settings "
"or chain position:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:184
msgid ""
"@ @error_ignore ls /no\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"# no exception\n"
"\n"
"@ echo 1 && @error_ignore ls /no && echo 2\n"
"  echo 3\n"
"1\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"3\n"
"# no exception; the `@error_ignore` also makes the chain\n"
"# treat the failing ls as \"don't raise on this final operand\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:198
msgid ""
"``@error_ignore`` is especially handy when you want a command to contribute "
"to the chain's return code but **not** its error behavior:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:201
msgid ""
"@ echo 1 | @error_ignore grep pattern | wc -l\n"
"0\n"
"# grep returns 1 (no match), @error_ignore keeps the pipe quiet,\n"
"# wc happily prints 0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:210
msgid "Catching the exception"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:212
msgid ""
"The raised exception is a plain ``subprocess.CalledProcessError``, so the "
"usual Python idioms work:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:215
msgid ""
"@ import subprocess\n"
"@ try:\n"
"      ls /no\n"
"  except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\n"
"      print(\"rc =\", e.returncode)\n"
"      print(\"cmd =\", e.cmd)\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"rc = 2\n"
"cmd = ['ls', '/no']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:227
msgid "For scoped overrides, ``env.swap`` is often cleaner than catching:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:229
msgid ""
"@ with @.env.swap(XONSH_SUBPROC_RAISE_ERROR=False):\n"
"      ls /no\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"# no exception, even though the default is True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:236
msgid ""
"For **captured iteration** there is also ``CommandPipeline.itercheck()`` "
"which raises ``XonshCalledProcessError`` (a subclass of ``subprocess."
"CalledProcessError`` that additionally carries ``.completed_command`` and ``."
"stderr``):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:241
msgid ""
"@ try:\n"
"      for line in !(grep -R TODO src/).itercheck():\n"
"          print(line.strip())\n"
"  except XonshCalledProcessError as e:\n"
"      print(\"grep failed with rc\", e.returncode)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:251
msgid "Interactive prompt behavior in detail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:253
msgid "With the default ``$XONSH_PROMPT_SHOW_SUBPROC_ERROR = False``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:255
msgid ""
"@ ls /no\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"@ echo $LAST_RETURN_CODE\n"
"2\n"
"\n"
"@ echo hi && ls /no && echo bye\n"
"hi\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"@"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:267
msgid "With ``$XONSH_PROMPT_SHOW_SUBPROC_ERROR = True``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:269
msgid ""
"@ $XONSH_PROMPT_SHOW_SUBPROC_ERROR = True\n"
"@ ls /no\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"subprocess.CalledProcessError: Command '['ls', '/no']' returned non-zero "
"exit status 2.\n"
"@"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:277
msgid "``@error_raise`` always shows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../error_handling.rst:279
msgid ""
"@ @error_raise ls /no\n"
"ls: cannot access '/no': No such file or directory\n"
"subprocess.CalledProcessError: Command '['@error_raise', 'ls', '/no']' "
"returned non-zero exit status 2."
msgstr ""

